COMPUTATIONAL STUDY OF COAXIAL ROTOR-FUSELAGE INTERACTIONS IN GROUND EFFECT
Aerospace Discipline: Aerodynamics Jonnadhula Sarika1, Dhwanil Shukla2 Indian Institute of Technology Bombay
Presenter Bio
Sarika Jonnadhula is a Master’s student in the Department of Aerospace Engineering at IIT Bombay, specializing in Aerodynamics. She received her Bachelor’s degree in Aerospace Engineering from Lakireddy Bali Reddy College of Engineering.Her current research focuses on rotor–fuselage interactions and ground effect phenomena in coaxial rotor systems.
Abstract
T
HIS study examines the aerodynamic performance of contra-rotating coaxial rotor helicopter operating in ground effect, with particular focus on rotor–fuselage–ground interactions. Simulations are conducted using DUST[8], a mid-fidelity vortex particle method solver with a Lagrangian framework. The geometric setup in DUST is illustrated in Figure 1, with coaxial rotors vertically separated by h = 0.25R, and the lower rotor positioned 0.1R above the ROBIN fuselage-a streamlined geometry. The test matrix (Figure 2) covers cases with and without fuselage, at ground proximities of Z/R = 1, 2, 3, and an isolated configuration without ground. Single rotor cases are also analyzed under identical conditions for comparison. Results reveal that fuselage presence enhances thrust—up to 12 N for coaxial and 9 N for single rotor setups at low Z/R—due to intensified rotor–fuselage interaction. Ground proximity also reduces downward force on the fuselage, highlighting the shielding effect of rotor wake near the ground.
Introduction
Coaxial rotor helicopters, characterized by two counter-rotating rotors mounted on the same axis, have gained considerable attention as an alternative to conventional single-rotor configurations. Originally developed to eliminate the need for a tail rotor, the coaxial system provides inherent torque balance, improved yaw control, and higher lifting efficiency within a compact footprint. This makes the design particularly attractive for applications requiring enhanced maneuverability, hovering efficiency, and reduced acoustic signature, such as urban air mobility and naval operations. Over the decades, both experimental studies and operational platforms—ranging from early Kamov designs to modern highperformance vehicles like the Sikorsky X2 and Raider—have demonstrated the potential of coaxial rotorcraft to achieve higher speeds and payload capacities compared to traditional counterparts. Despite these advantages, the aerodynamics of coaxial configurations are significantly more complex due to strong inter-rotor wake interactions, blade–vortex interactions, and their coupling with the fuselage flow field. These challenges are further exacerbated in ground effect, where the rotor wake interacts with the ground plane and fuselage, altering lift, induced power, and stability characteristics. Historically, rotorcraft aerodynamics has often been studied in isolation—focusing individually on rotor–fuselage interaction, coaxial rotor behavior, or ground effect. In the context of rotor–fuselage interaction, studies such as Berry and Bettschart [1] highlighted the importance of capturing unsteady pressure variations caused by rotor downwash, which is critical for accurate fuselage load predictions. Similarly, Steijl and Barakos [7] emphasized the azimuthal loading and upwash/downwash effects using CFD, showing how rotor wake alters fuselage flow field—a key phenomenon also present in coaxial systems.
