For coaxial rotor configurations, Cameron et al. [2] and Uehara et al. [9] investigated performance asymmetries and spacing effects, which directly influence wake interactions. Kim and Brown [4], using Vorticity Transport Models, demonstrated that optimal rotor spacing can minimize induced power losses, a crucial insight when studying the confined wakes of coaxial systems operating near fuselages and ground. In terms of ground effect, single rotor studies have been more comprehensive, with consistent findings that proximity to the ground increases lift and reduces power consumption. However, in coaxial rotors, the flow becomes more intricate. Silwal et al. [6] and Gibertini et al. [3] showed that ground effect benefits are primarily seen in the lower rotor, which then modifies the inflow to the upper rotor, often leading to unbalanced thrust. Despite these advancements, a significant research gap remains in analyzing coaxial rotor–fuselage interactions in ground effect within a unified computational framework. While rotor–fuselage, rotor–rotor, and rotor–ground interactions have each been explored individually, very few studies attempt to capture all three concurrently. A detailed literature review revealed that most prior work on coaxial systems in ground effect excludes the fuselage. Additionally, studies incorporating fuselage effects tend to investigate single rotors or quad-rotor UAVs, not coaxial helicopter configurations. As a result, this work represents one of the first comprehensive attempts to investigate integrated aerodynamic interactions of coaxial rotor systems with a fuselage near the ground : a scenario that closely reflects real-world low-altitude rotorcraft operations. To address this gap, the present study investigates the coupled aerodynamic effects of coaxial rotor–fuselage configurations operating in ground effect using DUST, a mid-fidelity, vortex particle method (VPM) solver [8]. Unlike prior studies, this work uniquely captures the combined influence in a single simulation framework. The rotors are modeled using lifting line theory, while the fuselage and ground are treated using a source-based panel method. As a representative real-world configuration, a fourbladed coaxial rotor system interacting with the streamlined ROBIN fuselage is simulated. This offers a more integrated depiction of rotorcraft behavior near the ground.
Numerical Approach
The present study utilizes the DUST software, which implements a mid-fidelity computational framework based on the Vortex Particle Method (VPM) for simulating rotorcraft aerodynamics. The fuselage is modeled using a source-based Panel Method, while the rotors are represented through the Lifting Line Method (LLM). The Vortex Particle Method is a mesh-free, Lagrangian approach particularly suited for modeling incompressible, high Reynolds number flows dominated by vorticity dynamics. It solves the Navier–Stokes equations in velocity–vorticity form:
Dω
Dt = (ω · ∇)u + ν∇2ω
where the vorticity field ω is defined as the curl of the velocity field u:
ω = ∇ × u.
Within this formulation, the flow is discretized into particles carrying vorticity, which are convected with the local velocity field. The mutual interactions among these particles govern the evolution of the velocity field, from which pressure gradients and aerodynamic forces can subsequently be determined. The Panel Method incorporated in DUST accounts for the inviscid influence of the fuselage by enforcing boundary conditions on the surface, while the Lifting Line Method captures the spanwise circulation distribution and induced velocities along the rotor blades. This provides an efficient framework to estimate aerodynamic loads and capture rotor–fuselage–ground interactions in coaxial rotorcraft.
Research Methodology
A coaxial rotor system with four blades per rotor was defined using the lifting line method, while the ROBIN fuselage and ground plane were modeled using a source-based panel method. The coaxial setup
