consists of two counter-rotating rotors, each with a radius(R) of 0.854m, linear twist of 6deg with zero pitch angle and NACA 0012 as airfoil section. The upper and lower rotors are vertically separated by 0.25R, and the lower rotor is located 0.1R above the fuselage centerline to simulate a realistic integrated configuration. A total of 10000 vortex particles were used per rotor based on a sensitivity study to ensure adequate resolution of the wake structures and also as a practical trade-off between computational cost and wake fidelity. The flat ground plane was defined as a rectangular surface with dimensions 6R×6R, large enough to avoid edge effects. The fuselage was positioned centrally and symmetrically aligned with the rotor hub. The hover condition was simulated by assigning a small positive vertical freestream velocity of 0.00001m/s for solver stability. The solver was advanced in time steps corresponding to 2.5deg of rotor rotation per step. Simulations were run for 20 rotor revolutions, which was determined from preliminary analysis as the point where wake structures stabilized and thrust variation became negligible. In postprocessing, wake topology is visualized using ParaView. Thrust and torque were extracted using Sectional load data which helps to compute the spanwise load distribution along blades or lifting surfaces.
Results and Discussions
As observed from Figure 3, thrust values converge in 15 rotations. Therefore, 20 rotations are selected for all cases. With these solver settings, the computed thrust shows a deviation of only 2.5% from the experimental results reported in [5], thereby validating the accuracy of the chosen settings. Simulations are performed for both the Single Rotor (SR) and Coaxial Counter-Rotating Rotor (CR) configurations. The wake structures in each case are visualized using ParaView (Figure 4). It is observed that the wake stabilizes within approximately 20 rotor revolutions. The SR configuration, consisting of a single main rotor and fuselage, produces an asymmetric and more diffused wake. A strong downward jet with pronounced swirl components is evident, primarily influenced by the unidirectional rotor rotation. In contrast, the CR configuration balances torque through counter-rotation of the blades, which mitigates wake asymmetry and leads to a more uniform flow structure. In the presence of the fuselage, the velocity contours(Figure 5) for the single rotor show a relatively more symmetric and streamlined wake. However, proximity to ground still causes the flow to spread radially along the fuselage surface, particularly at lower Z/R, resulting in noticeable wake distortion and enhanced local velocities near the nose. In the coaxial rotor configuration, the interaction between the upper and lower rotors again produces a more vertically aligned and intensified downwash with better flow integration over the fuselage. The reduced frontal area of the fuselage results in less wake separation and lower pressure buildup around the structure, allowing for a cleaner interaction with the coaxial-induced wake. Overall, the fuselage supports a more efficient wake development.
Performance analysis on Rotors:
Figure 6 illustrates the variation of rotor thrust (in N) and rotor torque (in N·mm) with respect to the non-dimensional height parameter Z/R, where Z is the distance from the ground and R is the rotor radius. It compares simulation results across different configurations: single rotor (with and without fuselage) and coaxial upper and lower rotors (with and without fuselage). For the coaxial configuration with fuselage, at Z/R = 1 the upper rotor produces approximately 58 N of thrust and 410 N·mm torque, while the lower rotor generates 55 N and 402 N·mm, respectively. Without fuselage, thrust drops to about 56 N (upper) and 53 N (lower), showing a fuselage-induced increase of 3.9% and 4.1%, respectively. At Z/R = 3, thrust differences between with- and withoutfuselage cases reduce to less than 1%. For the single rotor at Z/R = 1, thrust is 60 N with fuselage and 58 N without, an increase of 3.1%. The torque values are 417 N·mm and 404 N·mm, respectively, corresponding to a 3.2% increase. Across all configurations, most thrust reduction occurs between Z/R = 1 and Z/R = 2, beyond which values stabilize, confirming diminishing ground effect. Torque remains nearly constant with Z/R, indicating
