Performance analysis on Fuselage:
The pressure distribution over the upper and lower surfaces of the fuselage is illustrated in Figure 7. The fuselage, being symmetric about the XZ-plane, shows similar pressure behavior across both panels, with variations influenced by rotor configuration and ground proximity.The fuselage exhibits an initial increase in pressure near the nose region due to flow stagnation, which is clearly visible in both the CR and SR curves. The pressure then decreases smoothly toward the rotor hub location at X = 0.69 m, where the wake impinges on the surface, followed by relatively flat trends downstream. On the upper surface, pressure levels are more sensitive to the ground effect. On the lower surface, pressures remain consistently higher and less variable beyond the hub, with the CR configuration showing stronger ground-induced effects, especially at lower Z/R values. Overall, pressure differences between IGE and OGE are most pronounced in CR at Z/R = 1, highlighting enhanced rotor-fuselage-ground interactions in that regime. The graph (Figure 8) depicting the downward force on the fuselage as a function of Z/R shows a clear increase in force with increasing Z/R for both Coaxial Rotor (CR) and Single Rotor (SR) configurations. The CR consistently produces higher downward forces than the SR, following a similar trend as seen in other fuselage types. The force tends to plateau around Z/R = 3 to 4, indicating a stabilization of rotor-induced effects at larger distances.
Conclusion
Rotor–fuselage interactions in ground effect play a crucial role in rotorcraft performance, particularly at low altitudes (Z/R ≤ 2). The presence of the fuselage enhances rotor thrust by modifying the wake structure and increasing flow blockage, inducing ground effect-like conditions locally under the rotor. In coaxial rotor systems, especially over streamlined fuselages such as the ROBIN, the wake tends to remain more stable and vertically aligned compared to that of single-rotor configurations. Coaxial rotors consistently induce stronger downward forces on the fuselage due to intensified downwash, with these effects becoming less significant beyond (Z/R ≥ 3). Within coaxial systems, the lower rotor typically generates reduced thrust as a result of ground-induced velocities; however, this thrust asymmetry diminishes with increasing height.
Future Research Plans
The current work focuses on simulating the wake structure by explicitly modeling the ground plane in the computational domain. To capture the complete rotor-fuselage-ground interaction, future simulations involving forward flight should also be considered to extend the analysis beyond hover. This introduces additional complexities such as asymmetric inflow, advancing and retreating blade effects, and unsteady wake-fuselage interactions. These conditions will require refinement in both spatial and temporal discretization. Adding crosswind, terrain irregularities, or nearby obstacles (e.g., buildings, ship decks) in the simulation domain could enhance realism and applicability of the results to real-world rotorcraft operations. Future studies can incorporate blade-resolved CFD simulations to capture detailed blade-vortex and blade-fuselage interactions, especially near complex geometries. This would allow validation of wake models such as VPM against high-fidelity data.
